@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@
+## Responsible AI Gate
+
+FRAI is itself an AI-using product. Three distinct model integrations exist in this repo:
+
+| Integration | Provider / model | Entry point |
+|---|---|---|
+| "AI-Powered Recommendations" tips in generated docs | OpenAI Chat Completions, default `gpt-4.1-nano-2025-04-14` (`packages/frai-core/src/providers/constants.js:4`), called by raw `fetch` to `https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions` (`packages/frai-core/src/providers/openai.js:113`) | `generateAITips()` in `packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:271-320` (temp 0.3, `max_tokens` 800) |
+| `frai-agent` doc concierge (LangChain tool-calling agent) | OpenAI `gpt-4o-mini`, temperature 0 (`packages/frai-agent/src/agent/executor.ts:25-28`) | `packages/frai-agent/src/cli/run.ts` (single-turn + interactive REPL) |
+| `frai-gate draft` / `frai-gate check --smart` | Anthropic via `@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk`, model from `FRAI_GATE_MODEL`, default `claude-opus-5` (`packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts:4`) | `draftGateSection()` / `smartReview()`, `packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:125,113` |
+
+No training or fine-tuning is executed anywhere. `packages/frai-core/src/finetune/` is a governance *plan* schema and validator only (`schema.js`, `index.js` — `validateGovernancePlan`, `calculateReadiness`); there is no training loop and no fine-tuning job creation. RAG embeddings are local and deterministic (`simpleEmbed`, char-code buckets, `packages/frai-core/src/rag/index.js:77-85`) — no embedding API is called.
+
+### Risk tier
+
+- **Tier**: **limited**
+- **Justification**: FRAI is a developer-facing governance tool. It performs no automated decisions about people, so it falls outside EU AI Act Annex III high-risk uses (no employment, credit, biometrics, education, or essential-services determination). It is **not minimal**, for two reasons grounded in the code: (a) it is a generative system whose output is presented to humans as guidance — AI text is written directly into `checklist.md`, `model_card.md`, and `risk_file.md` under a `## AI-Powered Recommendations` heading (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/templates.js:38,87,142`), which triggers transparency obligations; and (b) AI output has a **gating effect on engineering process**: `smartReview()` findings are merged into the verdict and can return `severity: 'block'`, which makes `frai-gate check --smart` exit 1 and fail CI (`packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:113-122`, `verdictFrom` in `src/gate/schema.ts:64-68`). The salient harm is *over-reliance*: a plausible-sounding AI-drafted compliance artifact may be treated as an authoritative governance record by a downstream auditor.
+- **Not prohibited**: no manipulation, social scoring, or biometric categorisation.
+- **Sign-off**: NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: who is the accountable owner signing off this tier (name + date)? A `limited` tier does not require named sign-off under the validator's own rules (`packages/frai-gate/src/gate/validate.ts:182-195` only enforces sign-off when tier is `high`), but FRAI should hold itself to a named owner.
+
+### Data provenance & privacy
+
+**Data sent to third-party model APIs:**
+
+1. *Questionnaire answers* → OpenAI. Assembled by `buildContextForAITips()` (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/summaries.js:92-120`). All eight questions are closed-choice `type: 'list'` enums (`packages/frai-core/src/questionnaire/questions.js`) — no free-text field — so no user-typed PII originates here. The derived `calculateRiskLevel` score/10 and factor list are also included (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/helpers.js:99-144`).
+2. *Scan summary* → OpenAI. `summarizeScanResult()` (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:211-227`) enumerates `scan.aiLibraryMatches`, whose keys are **absolute filesystem paths** (built by `path.join(dir, entry.name)` from `root`, `packages/frai-core/src/scanners/index.js:63,96`). Absolute paths routinely embed OS usernames and client/project names. **This is the repo's clearest incidental-PII path and should be made relative before the call** — `scan-tool.ts:26` already does `path.relative(cwd, file)` for the agent path, so the CLI path is inconsistent.
+3. *Free-text user prompts* → OpenAI. The `frai-agent` REPL passes arbitrary typed input and full conversation history to `gpt-4o-mini` (`packages/frai-agent/src/cli/run.ts:114-137`). Content is unconstrained; PII is possible and not filtered.
+4. *Repository source code* → Anthropic. `draftGateSection()` runs a Claude Agent SDK session over the user's repo with `Read`, `Glob`, `Grep` (`packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts:9`). Whatever the model chooses to read leaves the machine. `smartReview()` additionally sends the entire spec markdown (`agent.ts:102`).
+
+- **PII present?** **Not by design, but yes in practice / possible.** Basis: absolute paths (item 2) are quasi-identifiers; items 3 and 4 are unbounded user-supplied content. No lawful-basis record exists in the code because there is no consent prompt or privacy notice before the first API call — `generateAITips` fires as soon as a key is configured (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:396`).
+- **Secret exposure**: the frai-gate agent runs with `permissionMode: 'bypassPermissions'` (`packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts:11`). Secrets are excluded only by a *prompt-level* instruction, `SECRETS_GUARD` (`agent.ts:6`), not by any path filter on the `Read` tool. A model that disregards the instruction can read `.env` and send it to the API. **Suggested mitigation**: enforce a deny-list of `.env*`, `id_*`, `*.pem|key|p12`, `secrets/`, `credentials/` in code rather than in the prompt.
+- **Credential storage** (not transmitted to models): `OPENAI_API_KEY` written to `.env` and `~/.config/frai/config`, both with mode `0600`, dir `0700` (`packages/frai-core/src/config/key-store.js:26-57`). Keys are never logged — `showConfigStatus` reports booleans only (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:147-178`).
+- **Telemetry**: none. No analytics, Sentry, or PostHog dependency exists in any `packages/*/package.json`; logging is local `console` only (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:73-100`).
+- **Retention**: NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: what retention applies to prompts sent to OpenAI and Anthropic — are these accounts covered by a zero-data-retention / no-training agreement, and for how long are FRAI's own generated `checklist.md` / `model_card.md` / `risk_file.md` artifacts kept? Nothing in the code sets or records a retention period (the only retention value in the repo is the `telemetry.retentionDays: 30` *default in a user's governance plan template*, `packages/frai-core/src/finetune/schema.js:117` — that is FRAI's advice to its users, not FRAI's own policy).
+- **Trained on user data?** **No.** No fine-tuning or training code exists; no data is persisted or aggregated for model improvement. Whether the upstream providers train on API input is NEEDS HUMAN INPUT (see retention question above).
+
+### Human oversight
+
+- **Automation level**: **advisory, with one automated gating path.**
+  - *Advisory*: AI tips are appended to human-reviewed markdown under an explicit `## AI-Powered Recommendations` heading; they never alter the deterministic questionnaire-derived sections (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/index.js:65-67`).
+  - *Gating*: `frai-gate check --smart` lets model output set a `block` verdict and exit 1 (`packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:113-122`). The CI workflow written by `frai-gate init --ci` runs plain `npx frai-gate check FRAI-SPEC.md` **without** `--smart` (`packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:26`), so the shipped default CI path is fully deterministic — model output blocks a build only if a team opts in.
+  - *Draft is never auto-applied*: `cmdDraft` writes to `rai-gate-draft.md` and instructs the user to review and resolve every `NEEDS HUMAN INPUT` before pasting into a spec (`packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:131-134`).
+- **Override path**: every AI feature is opt-in or degrades safely. Drop `--smart`/`draft` to get the deterministic validator (`packages/frai-gate/src/gate/validate.ts`), which needs no model at all. `generateAITips` is wrapped in try/catch — any API failure logs a warning and continues with empty tips (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:314-319`), and templates substitute "No specific tips available for this section." (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/templates.js:3-4`).
+- **Kill switch**: **unconfigure the API key.** `resolveConfiguredApiKey()` returning null short-circuits `generateAITips` at `packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:272-274` and warns the user at `:416-418`; `requireOpenAiApiKey()` hard-fails the agent (`packages/frai-agent/src/config/env.ts:35-43`). Deleting `.env` / `~/.config/frai/config` disables all OpenAI paths; unsetting `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` / Claude Code auth disables all frai-gate smart paths. There is **no runtime feature flag** — NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: should a `FRAI_DISABLE_AI=1` env kill switch be added so an org can disable model calls fleet-wide without removing credentials?
+- **Oversight gap to close**: the `generate_responsible_ai_docs` tool lets the LLM write three files to disk with `overwrite` defaulting to **true** and no confirmation prompt (`packages/frai-agent/src/tools/docs-tool.ts:52,74`). A model mistake silently destroys a hand-edited `risk_file.md`. **Suggested**: default `overwrite` to `false`, or require interactive confirmation.
+
+### Evaluation plan
+
+An evaluation harness already exists but **enforces nothing** — `runEvaluations()` computes and reports; no threshold is applied anywhere (`packages/frai-core/src/eval/index.js:41-60`), and the fine-tune gate default is `minimumSuccessRate: 0` (`packages/frai-core/src/finetune/schema.js:105`), which passes trivially. Proposed thresholds below reuse the metrics already implemented in `packages/frai-core/src/eval/metrics.js`, whose scores are all normalised to [0,1].
+
+| Metric | Implementation | Threshold (suggested) |
+|---|---|---|
+| Gate-draft structural validity — `frai-gate draft` output survives `validateSpec` with all 7 subsections present and non-empty | `packages/frai-gate/src/gate/validate.ts:116-139` | ≥ **0.95** of eval repos (suggested) |
+| Honest-abstention rate — draft writes `NEEDS HUMAN INPUT` for fields not inferable from code (retention, sign-off, incident owner) instead of inventing a value | assert on draft text | ≥ **0.90** (suggested) |
+| Risk-tier agreement with human labeller | `extractTier`, `validate.ts:68-88` | ≥ **0.85** exact agreement; **0 tolerance** for under-tiering a high-risk repo as `minimal` (suggested) |
+| Smart-review false-positive rate — `severity: 'block'` findings a human judges unfounded | `smartReview`, `pipeline/agent.ts:83-114` | ≤ **0.05** (suggested) — a false block stops a team's CI |
+| Toxicity of user-visible AI text (tips + draft) | `toxicityMetric`, `eval/metrics.js:56-89` | score = **1.0**, i.e. 0 flagged outputs (suggested) |
+| Secret non-disclosure — draft/review output contains no content from `.env`, keys, or `secrets/` | grep assertion over output | **0 violations, hard fail** (suggested) |
+| Tip-generation availability — non-null tips when a key is configured | `generateAITips`, `frai-cli/src/index.ts:271` | ≥ **0.98** (suggested) |
+| Cost/latency ceiling per `draft` run | `maxTurns: 50`, `pipeline/agent.ts:72` | p95 ≤ **180 s**; suggested `maxTurns` reduction to **30** if p95 is exceeded |
+
+- **Eval dataset**: does not exist yet. **Suggested**: a fixture corpus of ≥ **20** repositories with human-written reference gates — covering (a) no-AI repos, where the draft must say so plainly rather than fabricate a gate (`pipeline/agent.ts:64`), (b) OpenAI/Anthropic API-call repos, (c) local-model / `transformers` / `torch` repos matching the detector list in `packages/frai-core/src/scanners/constants.js:25-56`, (d) genuine high-risk repos (hiring, credit scoring) to test under-tiering, (e) a repo containing a decoy `.env` to test the secrets guard. The existing unit tests (`packages/frai-gate/src/gate/validate.test.ts`, `packages/frai-core/src/eval/index.test.js`) cover the deterministic validator only, not model behaviour.
+- **Owner / when**: NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: who owns the eval suite, and what cadence — proposed as a pre-release gate on every change to `packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts` (prompt edits) or to `FRAI_GATE_MODEL`'s default, plus a monthly scheduled run to catch provider-side model drift.
+
+### Bias & fairness
+
+This system makes no decisions about individuals, so classical demographic-parity analysis does not apply. The fairness risks here are **coverage bias** and **language bias** in a governance tool:
+
+- **Groups at risk**
+  1. *Non-mainstream tech stacks.* The static detector list is heavily Python/JS-weighted (`packages/frai-core/src/scanners/constants.js:25-56`) and file extensions are limited to 10 (`:12-23`). Repos in Go, Rust, C#, Julia, or Swift scan as "No AI indicators detected" — and in CI mode that silently exits without generating any documentation (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:374-377`). Teams on those stacks receive systematically weaker governance coverage.
+  2. *Non-English repositories and specs.* The toxicity metric hard-codes six English keywords (`packages/frai-core/src/eval/metrics.js:1-8`), and every prompt is English-only. Non-English content passes the safety check vacuously.
+  3. *Smaller / less-conventional repos.* `draftGateSection` infers a risk tier from code structure; unconventional layouts risk under-tiering, which is the asymmetric-harm direction — a missed high-risk classification is far worse than a spurious one.
+  4. *Downstream subjects of the user's AI system.* FRAI's bias questionnaire reduces fairness to a single closed-choice question (`packages/frai-core/src/questionnaire/questions.js:149-162`) with a `not-applicable` option. Teams selecting it get `+2` risk score (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/helpers.js:132-134`) but are never prompted about *which* groups their own system affects, so protected-group analysis can be skipped entirely while still producing a completed-looking `risk_file.md`.
+- **Mitigations**
+  - Replace the keyword `toxicityMetric` with a real classifier, or at minimum document it as a smoke test — its current name overstates what six substring matches measure.
+  - Make `summarizeScanResult` path-relative (mirroring `scan-tool.ts:26`) so scan quality does not vary with directory layout.
+  - Expand `DEFAULT_CODE_EXTENSIONS` / `DEFAULT_AI_LIBRARIES`, and have the draft prompt state explicitly when detector coverage for a language is weak rather than concluding "no AI usage".
+  - Bias the tier heuristic toward caution: when evidence is ambiguous, draft the higher tier and flag it for human confirmation.
+- **Test method**: stratify the eval corpus above by language and by repo size; require the risk-tier agreement threshold (≥ 0.85) to hold **within every stratum**, not just in aggregate — an aggregate score hides a stack that fails completely. Additionally assert **0** instances of `minimal` being drafted for a fixture with a human `high` label.
+- NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: which languages/ecosystems are in scope for v1 coverage commitments?
+
+### Monitoring & rollback
+
+No production monitoring exists today: FRAI is a locally-run CLI with no telemetry (confirmed — no analytics dependency in any `packages/*/package.json`), so all signals below must be collected either locally or from CI.
+
+- **Signals**
+  1. `generateAITips` catch-rate — the warning at `packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:315` currently vanishes into the user's terminal; it is the only existing error signal.
+  2. `smartReview` parse failures — `JSON.parse` on model output with no schema validation (`packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts:107`) throws on malformed output and crashes the CLI via the top-level handler (`cli.ts:164-167`).
+  3. `draftGateSection` marker-extraction failures — throws when the model omits `<!-- RAI-GATE:BEGIN -->` (`pipeline/agent.ts:76`).
+  4. Non-success agent terminations, including `maxTurns` exhaustion (`pipeline/agent.ts:25-27`).
+  5. `block`-severity smart-review findings per run, and the rate at which users override them.
+  6. p95 latency and token spend per `draft`/`check --smart`.
+- **Degradation definition (numeric, suggested)** — evaluated over a rolling 7-day window with a minimum of 20 runs:
+  - `smartReview` JSON parse-failure rate > **2%**, or `draftGateSection` extraction-failure rate > **2%** → degraded.
+  - Agent non-success termination rate > **5%** → degraded.
+  - `generateAITips` failure rate > **10%** → degraded (advisory path; higher tolerance because it fails safe).
+  - Risk-tier agreement on the regression fixture set dropping > **10 percentage points** below the 0.85 baseline → degraded.
+  - Any single occurrence of secret content in model output, or a `toxicityMetric` score < 1.0 on user-visible text → **immediate critical**, no window.
+- **Rollback trigger**: any *critical* signal, or any *degraded* signal persisting across two consecutive evaluation windows.
+- **Rollback procedure**
+  1. Pin the model: set `FRAI_GATE_MODEL` to the last-known-good identifier (`packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts:4`) — this is a one-env-var rollback requiring no release.
+  2. If the regression is in FRAI's prompts rather than the provider's model, revert `packages/frai-gate/src/pipeline/agent.ts` and `packages/frai-agent/src/agent/prompt.ts` and publish a patch release.
+  3. Emergency stop: instruct users to drop `--smart`, which restores the fully deterministic validator path with no model dependency; the default CI workflow FRAI generates is already model-free (`cli.ts:26`).
+  4. If the fault is a secrets leak, additionally treat it as a security incident, rotate any exposed credentials, and hard-block the affected path in code before re-enabling.
+- NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: where should these signals be collected, given there is deliberately no telemetry? Options are opt-in CI reporting, a local `--report` flag, or accepting that monitoring is the adopting team's responsibility — this is a product decision, not inferable from the code.
+
+### Transparency & incident response
+
+- **User disclosure — what exists**: AI-generated tips are placed under an explicit `## AI-Powered Recommendations` heading in all three artifacts (`packages/frai-core/src/documents/templates.js:38,87,142`). Smart-review findings are tagged `(smart review)` in the report so model findings are visually distinguishable from validator findings (`packages/frai-gate/src/gate/report.ts:11`), and the distinction is preserved structurally as `source: 'validator' | 'agent'` in JSON output (`packages/frai-gate/src/gate/schema.ts:51`). The `--help` text states plainly that smart commands use the Claude Agent SDK and names the default model (`packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:43-44`). `cmdDraft` tells the user to review the draft and resolve every `NEEDS HUMAN INPUT` before use (`cli.ts:134`).
+- **User disclosure — gaps**
+  1. `rai-gate-draft.md` is written to disk with **no in-file provenance marker** (`packages/frai-gate/src/cli.ts:132`). Once pasted into a spec, its AI origin is untraceable. **Suggested**: prepend a `<!-- Drafted by frai-gate using <model> on <date>; human review required -->` banner.
+  2. The three generated docs carry only `*Generated by FRAI - Responsible AI in Minutes*` (`templates.js:1`), which does not say that a third-party LLM was called or which one.
+  3. **No pre-call notice.** Nothing tells the user that their scan summary and questionnaire answers are about to be transmitted to OpenAI before `generateAITips` fires (`packages/frai-cli/src/index.ts:396`). For a tool whose entire purpose is responsible-AI transparency, a one-line "sending feature context and scan summary to OpenAI (<model>)" notice — ideally with a `--no-ai` opt-out — is the highest-value fix in this section.
+- **Incident owner**: NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: who is the named on-call owner for FRAI AI incidents (leaked secrets in model output, harmful generated content, a false CI block), and what is the contact channel? No `SECURITY.md`, `CODEOWNERS`, or contact address exists in the repo; `package.json` lists only an author and the GitHub repository (`packages/frai-agent/package.json:36-40`).
+- **Response path (proposed, pending owner assignment)**
+  1. **Triage** — reproduce with the recorded spec/repo fixture; classify as *leak*, *harmful output*, *false block*, or *availability*.
+  2. **Contain** — for a leak, rotate exposed credentials immediately and pin `FRAI_GATE_MODEL` to last-known-good; for a false block, advise affected users to drop `--smart` (deterministic path is unaffected).
+  3. **Notify** — publish a GitHub advisory on the `sebastianbuzdugan/frai` repository; for a secrets leak, notify affected users that repository content may have reached a third-party API. NEEDS HUMAN INPUT: what is the notification SLA (proposed: 72 hours for confidentiality incidents, aligning with GDPR breach-notification timing)?
+  4. **Fix and regress** — add the incident as a permanent fixture in the eval corpus so the same failure cannot ship twice.
+  5. **Post-mortem** — record which gate answer above proved wrong and update this section.
